For my imaginary dinner party, I'd invite an eclectic mix of fascinating historical figures:  

- Marie Curie, a pioneer in physics and chemistry;  
- Ada Lovelace, who wrote the world's first computer program; and  
- Albert Einstein, perhaps the most famous scientist of the 20th century.

These would make for riveting intellectual discussions. To round it out, I'd include:  

- Benjamin Franklin, the polymath who dabbled in just about everything;  
- Marie Antoinette, whose dramatic life fascinates me despite her personal failings;  
- Leonardo da Vinci, the quintessential Renaissance man with an insatiable curiosity;  

and perhaps for some lighter, more whimsical conversation:  

- Oscar Wilde, to debate aesthetics and philosophy at length.  

This combination offers diverse perspectives across several centuries and academic disciplines. The dinner would be a splendid way to explore both the intellectual and personal aspects of these iconic figures!
